\\log_{10}(19) + \\cdots + \\log_{10}(1)\n\\]\n\nUsing the known values of logarithms, we can calculate:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20) \\approx 1.3010, \\quad \\log_{10}(19) \\approx 1.2788, \\quad \\log_{10}(18) \\approx 1.2553, \\quad \\log_{10}(17) \\approx 1.2304, \\quad \\log_{10}(16) \\approx 1.2041\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(15) \\approx 1.1761, \\quad \\log_{10}(14) \\approx 1.1461, \\quad \\log_{10}(13) \\approx 1.1139, \\quad \\log_{10}(12) \\approx 1.0792, \\quad \\log_{10}(11) \\approx 1.0414\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(10) = 1, \\quad \\log_{10}(9) \\approx 0.9542, \\quad \\log_{10}(8) \\approx 0.9031, \\quad \\log_{10}(7) \\approx 0.8451, \\quad \\log_{10}(6) \\approx 0.7782\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(5) \\approx 0.6990, \\quad \\log_{10}(4) \\approx 0.6021, \\quad \\log_{10}(3) \\approx 0.4771, \\quad \\log_{10}(2) \\approx 0.3010, \\quad \\log_{10}(1) = 0\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 18.3823\n\\]\n\nThis means \\(20!\\) is approximately \\(10^{18.3823}\\), which is a number slightly larger than \\(10^{18}\\) and less than \\(2 \\times 10^{18}\\). Therefore, \\(20!\\) is a 19-digit number starting with a digit between 1 and 1.9999, implying the first digit is 2.\n\nTo confirm, we can use the given information about the last 18 digits. If the last 18 digits are 432902008176640000, the number \\(20!\\) can be written as \\(N \\times 10^{18}\\) where \\(1 \\leq N < 2\\). Therefore, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is 2.\n\nThus, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine the first digit of \\(20!\\), we start by noting that \\(20!\\) is a large number with 19 digits, and the last 18 digits are given as 432902008176640000. This means the last 18 digits of \\(20!\\) are 432902008176640000, and we need to find the first digit.\n\nFirst, let's estimate the magnitude of \\(20!\\) using logarithms. The logarithm (base 10) of \\(20!\\) can be approximated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) = \\log_{10}(20 \\times 19 \\times \\cdots \\times 1) = \\log_{10}(20) + \\log_{10}(19) + \\cdots + \\log_{10}(1)\n\\]\n\nUsing the known values of logarithms, we can calculate:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20) \\approx 1.3010, \\quad \\log_{10}(19) \\approx 1.2788, \\quad \\log_{10}(18) \\approx 1.2553, \\quad \\log_{10}(17) \\approx 1.2304, \\quad \\log_{10}(16) \\approx 1.2041\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(15) \\approx 1.1761, \\quad \\log_{10}(14) \\approx 1.1461, \\quad \\log_{10}(13) \\approx 1.1139, \\quad \\log_{10}(12) \\approx 1.0792, \\quad \\log_{10}(11) \\approx 1.0414\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(10) = 1, \\quad \\log_{10}(9) \\approx 0.9542, \\quad \\log_{10}(8) \\approx 0.9031, \\quad \\log_{10}(7) \\approx 0.8451, \\quad \\log_{10}(6) \\approx 0.7782\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(5) \\approx 0.6990, \\quad \\log_{10}(4) \\approx 0.6021, \\quad \\log_{10}(3) \\approx 0.4771, \\quad \\log_{10}(2) \\approx 0.3010, \\quad \\log_{10}(1) = 0\n\\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(20!) \\approx 18.3823\n\\]\n\nThis means \\(20!\\) is approximately \\(10^{18.3823}\\), which is a number slightly larger than \\(10^{18}\\) and less than \\(2 \\times 10^{18}\\). Therefore, \\(20!\\) is a 19-digit number starting with a digit between 1 and 1.9999, implying the first digit is 2.\n\nTo confirm, we can use the given information about the last 18 digits. If the last 18 digits are 432902008176640000, the number \\(20!\\) can be written as \\(N \\times 10^{18}\\) where \\(1 \\leq N < 2\\). Therefore, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is 2.\n\nThus, the first digit of \\(20!\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).","id":"number_theory:445"}
